Here’s what was wrong and how to fix it.

### Problems in the original script  

| Issue | Why it’s an error |
|-------|-------------------|
| `document.getAnimations("t3").value` | There is no such method on `document`. You meant `document.getElementById("t3").value`. |
| Using `num1`, `num2`, `num3` instead of the variables you actually stored (`n1`, `n2`, `n3`). | Those names don’t exist, so the code throws a *ReferenceError*. |
| Comparing **strings** (`n1 > n2`) instead of **numbers**. | When the inputs are read as strings, `"10"` is lexicographically larger than `"9"` – you’ll get wrong results. Convert the values to numbers first. |
| Missing numeric conversion (`parseFloat`/`Number`). | Same reason as above. |
| The `if‑else` chain could miss the case where two numbers are equal. | Using `>=` makes the logic clearer (or you can keep `>` and add an explicit tie‑break). |

### Corrected version

```html
<p>Write a program to find the largest number among 3 numbers.</p>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 1st number" id="t1">
<br>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 2nd number" id="t2">
<br>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 3rd number" id="t3">
<button onclick="check()">Check</button>
<h3 id="ans">The largest number is</h3>

<script>
function check() {
    // 1️⃣ Grab the three inputs and turn them into real numbers
    let n1 = Number(document.getElementById("t1").value);
    let n2 = Number(document.getElementById("t2").value);
    let n3 = Number(document.getElementById("t3").value);

    // 2️⃣ Determine which one is the biggest
    let result;
    if (n1 >= n2 && n1 >= n3) {
        result = "The largest is " + n1;
    } else if (n2 >= n3) {
        result = "The largest is " + n2;
    } else {
        result = "The largest is " + n3;
    }

    // 3️⃣ Show the answer
    document.getElementById("ans").innerHTML = result;
}
</script>
```

#### What changed?

1. **Correct element retrieval** – `document.getElementById("t3").value` instead of the bogus `getAnimations`.
2. **Numeric conversion** – `Number(...)` (or `parseFloat`) ensures the comparisons work on numbers, not strings.
3. **Use the right variable names** – `n1`, `n2`, `n3` are now used consistently.
4. **Fixed the `if‑else` logic** – now correctly picks the largest value and stores the whole sentence in `result`.
5. **Display the result** – `innerHTML` receives the complete string built in `result`.

You can paste the whole block (HTML + script) into a page and it will work as expected. If you want to handle empty inputs gracefully, you could add a quick guard like:

```js
if (isNaN(n1) || isNaN(n2) || isNaN(n3)) {
    document.getElementById("ans").innerHTML = "Please enter valid numbers.";
    return;
}
```

but the core fix above resolves the syntax/runtime errors you were seeing. Happy coding!

